course: dictionary
lesson: 3942
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one you need. «The cup is full» means it holds as much as it can hold. «I'm full» means you have eaten enough. The words around full give you the clue.
explain: Full can also mean whole or complete. «Your full name» means every part of your name, not just your first name. «A full hour» means all sixty minutes, not forty-five.
explain: Full often joins with other words. «Full of» means having a lot of something, as in «full of bad air». Try each meaning in the sentence. The one that still makes sense is the right one.
fact: Full means holding as much as it can hold, as in «a full cup».
fact: Full means having eaten enough, as in «I'm full». Casual words for this are «stuffed» and «I'm done».
fact: Full means complete or whole, as in «your full name» or «a full hour».
fact: «Full of» means having a lot of something: «Never go into old mines, caves or tunnels; they can collapse or be full of bad air.»
fact: «Full immersion» means going right under the water, as in «usually by full immersion».
exercise: «The cup is full.» What does full mean here? | Lots of mistakes | *Holding as much as it can hold | Very intense | Having eaten enough
exercise: «Never go into old mines; they can be full of bad air.» What does full of mean? | *Having a lot of something | Complete and whole | Having eaten enough | Very fast
exercise: «He gave his full name at the desk.» What does full mean here? | Having eaten enough | *Complete and whole | Very hungry | Holding as much as it can hold
test: «After the big dinner, Sam said, "I'm full."» What does full mean? | Very fast | *Having eaten enough | Holding as much water as it can hold | Complete and whole
test: Mia has a full hour for her project. How long is that? | *Sixty minutes, all of it | Forty-five minutes | Thirty minutes
exam: «When you flush, a float rises as the cistern refills and shuts off the water when it is full.» What does full mean here? | Complete and whole | *Holding as much water as it can hold | Very intense | Having eaten enough
